      {
        "id": 7038,
        "name": "flamethrower49",
        "username": "flamethrower49",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/f/838e76/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2013-01-12T09:36:25.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>The situation: The final turns of a Mad Bomber Blade game, set in the Realm of Discord. &nbsp;Argent Adept, Fanatic, and Tempest were hanging on, looking for a way to win. &nbsp;It didn't look good. &nbsp;Argent Adept led the pack with 5 hit points, Fanatic and Tempest were at 2. &nbsp;Our collective stuff totaled one Alacritous Subdominant, and one Aegis of Resurrection. &nbsp;Maniacal Death Ray Blade had a hearty 40, along with a Living Force Field and a Backlash Field. &nbsp;The latter was actually what had dealt the most damage to Tempest and Fanatic as they negotiated his first side as fast as possible.</p><p>Now, Tempest had a Ball Lightning, the first ongoing destruction we had found all game. &nbsp;But he also had a problem. &nbsp;Ball Lightning deals four damage to one target first, and Blade... could not be that target. &nbsp;Tempest would die to the Backlash damage, and we wouldn't get rid of the ongoings. &nbsp;When Argent Adept powered Alacritous Subdominant, I suggested something crazy. &nbsp;I said we aim the damage at Argent Adept. &nbsp;That way, he would not be the highest target when Blade fired up his Death Ray. &nbsp;He would instead fire on Fanatic, who would shrug it off with the Aegis and then we could finish him the next turn. &nbsp;</p><p>They thought I was nuts, but they went with it. &nbsp;Tempest took Argent Adept down to 1, and zapped the ongoings. &nbsp;Fanatic lit Blade up with Wrathful Retribution for 28, and Exorcism for 2. Tempest Reclaimed a Lightning Slash for the next turn, and drew another, taking Blade down to 9 with Squall. &nbsp;(And incidentally retrieving Wrathful Retribution for Fanatic, if she were going to get another chance to use it.) &nbsp;The environment turned up... a Portal Fiend! &nbsp;Irrelevant!</p><p>Then Blade went. &nbsp;He blew through Fanatic's Aegis, as predicted, and then played two cards that didn't do any damage. &nbsp;Tempest and Fanatic gleefully finished him off, exulting in the wake of a plan crazy enough to work. &nbsp;There were so many things that could have gone wrong with that, especially given the environment. &nbsp;I can't say that was ironclad strategy or anything, but it was sure as hell fun.</p>",
        "post_number": 1,
        "post_type": 1,
